Chksum is a little program that I've developed and used a lot over theyears. The prime difference between it and the ones that it copies, isthat it can read a list of pathnames from stdin.  This turns out to bevery handy indeed. Also, it uses a 32-bit CRC, which means that a falsematch is 2**16 times less likely than with the 16-bit brands.  Itproduces identical results on both PCs and VAXen.The 32-bit CRC routine was donated by kind souls on the net.  I havetweaked it for my use, principally by adding inline optimized assemblycode for my PC. I don't pretend to know what is going on inside, and Iregard it as a minor miracle that it actually produces the same numberson PCs as on VAXen.  Perhaps somebody will tell me if it produces the"right" numbers.I've developed it on an Aztec C compiler, currently version 4.10a.  Youshouldn't have much trouble porting it to your favorite compiler.  Ihave found that on the VAX I had to comment-out the #asm stuff incrc32.c, in spite of the fact that it is already conditionally compiledout.  I have stubbed-out my debugging stuff, so the printd()'s andinitdebug() won't produce any code.If you don't have string.h, then you probably don't have the stringlibrary either.  No sweat, all you need do is delete the include forstring.h, and add the following routine to chksum.c:       local void strcpy(dst, src)       char *dst, *src;               {               while ((*dst++ = *src++) != '\0')                       continue;               }You'll need to remove the reference to the library in the makefile too.If all else fails, the DOS executable is now available at finecomp.binaries.pc  newsgroups everywhere.I hereby donate this program to the Public Domain for any and allpurposes. Enjoy.Here is the output of "chksum -v *.h *.c makefile":26b5c1c7         436   debug.h02180ae4        2837   gen.h872b0f18        8136   chksum.c6d239b68       11016   crc32.c44bdd9f4         536   makefile=======